;; A yield point inside a `(do ...)` block over `List`.
;;
;; `:bind` desugars to a call to `bind` with the rest of the block as an
;; ordinary lambda, and whether that lambda survives depends on the monad.
;;
;; `Option`'s `bind` mentions its continuation once, so trait inlining
;; substitutes it and beta reduction leaves straight-line code — which is why
;; the same shape is *allowed* in `074_bjoroutine.bjo`. `List`'s `bind` calls its
;; continuation once per element, the occurrence check refuses to substitute a
;; term used more than once, and the lambda is still there when the colour check
;; runs. A suspension inside it has nowhere to live.
;;
;; The design's §8.5 asks for a message naming the monad and saying why. It
;; names `list-foldr` and the arrow its callback is declared with, which is one
;; step short: that is the function `bind` desugared *into*, not the `List`
;; whose instance chose it. Naming the monad means carrying provenance through
;; trait inlining.
;;
;; What changed when `list-foldr` gained a `-?->` callback is which half is at
;; fault. The parameter is no longer the obstacle — it takes either colour — so
;; the lambda is, and the message says so. That fix is one word for anyone who
;; wrote the lambda, which is why it is offered plainly; this fixture is the
;; case where nobody did, and the second sentence is there for it.

;; EXPECT-ERROR: calling 'fetch' is a yield point
;; EXPECT-ERROR: yield point is not allowed here
;; EXPECT-ERROR: passed to 'list-foldr', whose parameter is declared -?-> and so takes either colour
;; EXPECT-ERROR: Where the lambda is not one you wrote, whatever built it decides

(import (std prelude))
(import (std monad))

(: fetch (-> int int))
(defbjo (fetch x) (* x 2))

(: multi (-> int (List int)))
(defbjo (multi n)
  (do (:bind x (Cons n (Cons (+ n 1) Nil)))
      (:return (fetch x))))

(defun (main args) 0)
